        "text": "{\"scores\": {\"center\": 5, \"craft\": 4, \"facts\": 4, \"age_fit\": 5, \"continuity\": 4, \"delight\": 4},\n\"evidence\": {\"center\": \"Lennon acts in every chapter. He volleys the feather ball through the cuju hole, dribbles past 'three farmers and a very confused goose', calls the 1863 vote, takes the 'first' penalty against McCrum, grabs the flickering 1930 trophy, unwraps Pickles's bundle and scores the final top-corner penalty. His penalty practice and his penalty shootout game shape both the cold open and the climax. Level 6 is the thinnest: 'Not on my watch' is his only real contribution there.\", \"craft\": \"The story has a strong shape. It cold-opens on backyard penalties, the ball and fence make a clear portal, the 'three steps back' callback runs through the climax, and the pixel message makes a good keepsake at home. However, the flicker-save-ding beat repeats almost unchanged level after level. In Level 6, Lennon's 'Not on my watch' promises action and nothing follows, so the coin drops without any challenge.\", \"facts\": \"Most facts are accurate and well chosen: Freemasons' Tavern 1863, the two balls in the 1930 final, Gaetjens, Pickles, Ken Aston's traffic-light cards, the Dick, Kerr Ladies ban. There are a few slips. McCrum stamps 'a spot twelve yards out' and Lennon uses 'the very first penalty spot in history', but in 1891 the kick was taken from a 12-yard line and the spot came in 1902. The net-with-a-hole cuju belongs to a later dynasty than 'over 2,000 years ago'. The 'typo' newspaper legend is stated without a hedge. The True Facts list matches the story.\", \"age_fit\": \"The 2633 words sit well within the 2200-3200 range. The dry, self-aware humour suits a 10-year-old: 'That's the most sensible silly idea I've ever heard', 'Okay, that one doesn't count,' he told nobody. The mob stampede is mild peril, resolved at once.\", \"continuity\": \"Bit, the golden coins fusing into a trophy, 'Player One', 'BZZZT', 'Level complete' and Bit's watery eyes all return naturally. The bible says Bit jokes about being 8-bit, but he never does. The opening mentions the video-game adventure only in general terms and does not pick up the last ending ('THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD').\", \"delight\": \"There are lovely read-aloud touches: the CLANG off the gutter, the grumpy '...aye', 'Seriously. The best boy.' But the final line 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' is printed twice, once before and once after 'The End', which reads as a glitch. Some of Bit's explanations drift into lecture.\"},\n\"caps_applied\": [\"none\"],\n\"disqualified\": false,\n\"overall\": 4.35,\n\"editor_notes\": [\"Give Lennon a real job in Level 6 (for example, keeping a flickering ball alive for the US team, or rescuing the scoreline), or cut 'Not on my watch'. Right now the setup has no payoff and it is his most passive chapter.\", \"Tighten the facts: in 1891 the penalty was taken from a 12-yard line, and the spot arrived in 1902. Hedge the English 'typo' newspaper story as a legend. Either move the net-with-a-hole cuju to a later era or describe the earlier goals.\", \"Delete the duplicated 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' after 'The End'. Add one 8-bit joke for Bit, and let the opening echo the last story's 'the next level is yours to build' so series fans feel the handoff.\"],\n\"best_moment\": \"\\\"No kicking people in the shins?\\\" A long pause. Then, grumpily: \\\"...aye.\\\"\",\n\"worst_moment\": \"Lennon cracked his knuckles. \\\"Not on my watch.\\\"\"}"
